  weight and if she didn't want the pasta  she could easily add in some quinoa or  rice sweet potato or just double down on  the fruit to make it really super easy  we ideally want to get like a one to  four ratio of protein to carbs after a  workout to help replenish those glycogen  stores and help drive protein into the  muscle faster so i would say if she were  to really focus on carbs anywhere in her  day it should be here it's still a  couple more hours till cousin goes to  sleep so i'm needing a little boost of  energy i got some grapes these are a  great quick source of energy as they  have fructose in them um so they're a  little bit higher in the simple sugars  but they're a great quick source of  energy and then i'm having a little less  than a handful of nuts and this is a  little bit of a longer lasting source of  energy and by a little bit i mean a lot  more first of all i have no qualms about  snacks  i i think snacks are a really important  way to help you get the nutrients that  you need in the day especially for  highly active people like joe but i do  think that had we had more carbs and  more calories in that post-workout meal  that we'd actually likely have a bit  more energy to perhaps get us to dinner  now while protein is king in the satiety  hierarchy like i said we really can't  forget that carbs are quite literally  energy and yes we can derive energy from  fat allah ketosis but there is nothing  quite like the og now having said that i  think that this is a really beautiful  balanced snack we've got some protein  healthy fats and fiber from the nuts  plus some extra energizing carbs and  fiber in the grapes but i gotta say  like do we really  need to constantly label foods as simple  carbs or complex carbs feels exhausting  getting sleepy huh  no while she's not wrong in pointing out  that grapes contain fructose is it  really that helpful to narrow our focus  down to this singular food component  because it's kind of missing the forest  from the trees and by trees i mean  antioxidants fiber energy flavor and fun  i really don't think we need another  good food versus bad food dichotomy to  have to obsess over especially if it  means demonizing something as nutritious  as fruit so it's dinner time today i  made  some sweet potato fries  i am sauteing some brussels sprouts here  and then i also made some chicken  tenderloins and i made a little sauce  basically just ketchup honey and  sriracha so it's like a sweet and sour  and spicy  [Music]  okay so we've got another solid family  meal here with some fiber-rich carbs  from the sweet potato we got protein  from the chicken and some veggie and  fiber action from the brussels sprouts  now it is a little low in fat as i'm not  sure if she's cooking with any oil in  there i know dieters are traditionally  quite hesitant to cook in oils or fats  but i would say two teaspoons spread  over two people is only like 40 calories  a person but it adds so much to the  texture flavor and satisfaction factor  obviously i think we can be smart about  added oil especially in the context of  weight loss but we don't need to  completely cut it out now aside from  that i do think it seems like a little  bit confusing that joe was so adamant to  call out every source of simple sugar in  this video while coating the chicken in  ketchup and honey i have nothing against  ketchup or honey i mean we eat ketchup  with our side fries in my house but  there's just a lot of mixed messages  going on and i really don't think joe  needs to worry so much about the whole  simple versus complex carb situation  when incorporating it into balanced  meals like this if you find that you are  struggling with eating the right things  or fueling yourself properly is  something that you can do is look up  complex carbs and healthy fats so  complex carbs are the ones that you want  to lean on a lot of people if they're  asking if you're asking them how do i  lose weight how do i lose fat they'r